<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kairouan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kairouan is a city in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, founded in the seventh century as an early Arab Muslim centre and a seat of religious learning. Around 1040 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudala">Gudala</a> chieftain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-ibrahim">Yahya ibn Ibrahim</a> met the jurist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-imran-al-fasi">Abu Imran al-Fasi</a> there. This meeting led to the mission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-ibn-yasin">Abdallah ibn Yasin</a> and the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kairouan.jpg" alt="Kairouan" width="306" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ELEL09, montage et assemblage · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montage_ville_de_Kairouan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After pacifying the country, Ibrahim founded a new capital, al-Abbasiyya, outside <b>Kairouan</b> in 800 and built it between 801 and 810.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…lay around 1035–1040 CE, when Yahya ibn Ibrahim, a chieftain of the Godala (Gudala) tribe returning from pilgrimage to Mecca, met the Fez-born Maliki jurist Abu Imran al-Fasi in <b>Kairouan</b> and was eventually provided with a teacher, Abdallah ibn Yasin, to instruct his people in orthodox Maliki practice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Marinid sultan Abu al-Hasan occupied Tunis in 1347, but his defeat by Arab tribes near <b>Kairouan</b> in 1348 ended the occupation, and a second Marinid invasion in 1357 was also short-lived.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the west, Uqba ibn Nafi founded <b>Kairouan</b> around 670, North Africa was subdued, and in 711 Tariq ibn Ziyad crossed into Spain, beginning the Umayyad conquest of Hispania and overthrowing the Visigothic kingdom; raids beyond the Pyrenees were checked by Charles Martel at the Battle of Tours…</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Al-Andalus was governed by walis appointed from <b>Kairouan</b> or Damascus, first from Seville and from around 716 from Córdoba.</blockquote><a href="/state/al-andalus-umayyad-province">Al-Andalus (Umayyad province)</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Banu Hilal defeated the Zirid emirate and <b>Kairouan</b> was abandoned in 1057, Tunis broke away.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-khurasan">Banu Khurasan</a></li></ul></section>
